From: Alex <xnderv@yandex.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/nouveau/therm: mask the update bit when reading back the fan PWM duty

nv50_fan_pwm_set() and gf119_fan_pwm_set() write the duty cycle to 0xe118
with bit 31 set. Read back right after such a write, the register still
has bit 31 set, and nv50_fan_pwm_get()/gf119_fan_pwm_get() return it as
part of the duty cycle. nvkm_fanpwm_get() then takes divs = max(divs, duty),
so divs becomes 0x8000xxxx too, and duty * 100 overflows u32
(0x80000000 * 100 == 0 mod 2^32): the fan reads as 0%. nvkm_fan_update()
uses that as the current speed and steps from it, so the fan is set to 3%
and climbs back by 3% per bump period.

The writer and the reader are the two nouveau timers: the 1 s thermal poll
(nvkm_therm_alarm) and the fan bump alarm (nvkm_fan_alarm). When the target
moves by more than one 3% step, the second bump lands on the next poll tick;
one of them writes 0xe118 and the other reads it a few microseconds later.

Seen on a GTX 560 Ti (GF114, nv50 PWM path) under a load that makes the
temperature swing quickly: traced with bpftrace, 7 of 239 duty reads in two
minutes came 7-25 us after the other timer's write, with bit 31 still set
(e.g. 0x800001e1 with divs 0x21c), and each was followed by a drop to 3%.

gm107_fan_pwm_get() and the PTHERM PWM path of gf119_fan_pwm_get() already
mask their duty reads; do the same for 0xe118.

---
diff --git a/nvkm/subdev/therm/nv50.c b/nvkm/subdev/therm/nv50.c
--- a/nvkm/subdev/therm/nv50.c
+++ b/nvkm/subdev/therm/nv50.c
@@ -72,7 +72,11 @@
 
 	if (nvkm_rd32(device, ctrl) & (1 << line)) {
 		*divs = nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00e114 + (id * 8));
-		*duty = nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00e118 + (id * 8));
+		/*
+		 * Bit 31 is the update trigger set by pwm_set();
+		 * it can still read back as set after a write.
+		 */
+		*duty = nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00e118 + (id * 8)) & 0x7fffffff;
 		return 0;
 	}
 
diff --git a/nvkm/subdev/therm/gf119.c b/nvkm/subdev/therm/gf119.c
--- a/nvkm/subdev/therm/gf119.c
+++ b/nvkm/subdev/therm/gf119.c
@@ -74,7 +74,11 @@
 	else if (indx < 2) {
 		if (nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00d610 + (line * 0x04)) & 0x00000040) {
 			*divs = nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00e114 + (indx * 8));
-			*duty = nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00e118 + (indx * 8));
+			/*
+			 * Bit 31 is the update trigger set by pwm_set();
+			 * it can still read back as set after a write.
+			 */
+			*duty = nvkm_rd32(device, 0x00e118 + (indx * 8)) & 0x7fffffff;
 			return 0;
 		}
 	} else if (indx == 2) {
